{"entries":{"chapter-60-study-behavioral-symptoms-of-dementia-and-psychoactive-drug-therapy|Chapter 60 Study: Behavioral Symptoms of Dementia and Psychoactive Drug Therapy":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Neuropsychiatric symptoms (NPS) occur in up to 98% of dementia patients over the course of illness (p919); depression, apathy, and anxiety are the most common NPS in dementia (p919).":["u3"],"copy|New late-life NPS lasting over 6 months, without dementia and not explained by another psychiatric disorder = mild behavioral impairment (MBI) (p919).":["u4"],"copy|NPS → higher mortality, faster cognitive decline, and are a top driver of institutionalization.":["u5"],"copy|4 NPS domains: affective/motivational, psychotic, disturbances of basic drives (sleep/eating), and disinhibited/socially inappropriate behavior.":["u6"],"copy|Get a reliable informant history — the patient often cannot self-report accurately (p920).":["u7"],"copy|Rule out delirium first for any acute or fluctuating change in cognition/behavior (p923).":["u8"],"copy|NPI = most-used research tool for NPS; NPI-Q = brief clinic version, measures 12 NPS domains plus informant distress (p922).":["u9"],"copy|Medication review is essential: the anticholinergic-risk/delirium med list (Table 60-3, p922) includes non-obvious agents digoxin and furosemide alongside the classic anticholinergics (TCAs, diphenhydramine/hydroxyzine/promethazine, oxybutynin, scopolamine).":["u10"],"copy|New-onset acute NPS work-up: CBC, chem panel, LFTs, UA/culture; consider TSH, B12, folate, tox screen, ECG; chest x-ray if respiratory signs; imaging particularly if focal signs; EEG if seizure suspected; CSF rarely needed — indicated if meningitis/encephalitis suspected (p922).":["u11"],"copy|Depression affects up to 50% of dementia patients and may go undetected — in dementia it is more likely to present with agitation, irritability, and anxiety (p923).":["u12"],"copy|Psychosis occurs in 20% to 30% of AD patients vs over 50% of DLB/Parkinson disease dementia patients; visual hallucinations (more common in DLB) may be the most clinically useful feature to distinguish DLB from AD (p923–924).":["u13"],"copy|DICE approach: Describe, Investigate, Create, Evaluate (p924).":["u14"],"copy|MIND at Home study: 99% of community-dwelling dementia patients had unmet care needs (p924); 60% to 70% of older adults with AD and other dementias live in the community, cared for by family and friends (p924).":["u15"],"copy|In the US about 25% of people with dementia receive care from nonfamily caregivers (eg, home health aides), who may not always receive the necessary dementia-care training (p925).":["u16"],"copy|Nonpharmacologic interventions are first-line — exception is emergency situations where the safety of the patient or others is compromised (eg, severe agitation) (p925).":["u17"],"copy|Psychotropics only after nonpharmacologic approaches fail, EXCEPT 3 cases: clear-cut major depression; psychosis causing/risking harm; aggression causing/risking harm (p925).":["u18"],"copy|Psychotropics are routinely prescribed despite safety/efficacy concerns: in newly admitted nursing-home residents, only 12% of new admissions got nonpharmacologic treatment in the first 3 months, while 71% received at least one psychotropic (p927).":["u19"],"copy|No significant benefit after a 4-week period → taper and withdraw; reassess at least monthly while tapering and for at least 4 months after stopping (p927).":["u20"],"copy|For significant delirium-related agitation requiring short-term medication, the chapter gives these low PRN doses at six-hour intervals (p923). Haloperidol 0.25–0.5 mg orally (IV only with appropriate monitoring), quetiapine 12.5–25 mg orally, olanzapine 2.5 mg orally or 1.25–2.5 mg IM, or risperidone 0.25–0.5 mg orally. There is no approved IV risperidone formulation.Current practice (not the book): risperidone is not given IV; Risperdal Consta is IM only — \"Do not administer intravenously\" — Risperdal Consta prescribing information (Janssen).":["u21"],"copy|Risperidone 0.25–1 mg/day — most effective for psychosis and agitation per AHRQ review; dose-dependent EPS, ↑cerebrovascular risk (p928).":["u22"],"copy|Olanzapine 2.5–10 mg/day — most effective for agitation per AHRQ review; sedation, weight gain, glucose effects (p928).":["u23"],"copy|Quetiapine 12.5–150 mg/day — sedation, orthostatic hypotension; with clozapine, less likely than other antipsychotics to worsen parkinsonian symptoms (p928).":["u24"],"copy|Aripiprazole 5–10 mg/day — most effective for overall NPS per AHRQ review; lower EPS, nausea (p928).":["u25"],"copy|Haloperidol 0.5–5 mg/day — high EPS risk; should not be a first-line neuroleptic in nonemergent situations (p928). Retrospective cohorts: higher mortality than risperidone, olanzapine, aripiprazole, ziprasidone, or quetiapine (p927).":["u26"],"copy|Clozapine 6.25–75 mg/day — low EPS risk, so a good option for psychotic symptoms in DLB (DLB/PD patients are extremely EPS-sensitive, except to clozapine); limited by neutropenia/agranulocytosis — weekly blood draws in the first 6 months (p928).":["u27"],"copy|Pimavanserin 34 mg/day — FDA-approved for Parkinson disease psychosis; confusion, edema, hypotension (p928); Current practice (not the book): brexpiprazole received FDA approval in May 2023 for agitation associated with Alzheimer dementia; its boxed warning states increased mortality in older patients with dementia-related psychosis treated with antipsychotics - AGS Alternatives 2025, Table 3 footnote e, p12; FDA announcement, 11 May 2023, approval and boxed-warning paragraphs":["u28"],"copy|Citalopram — initial 5–10 mg, max 20 mg/day; CitAD trial showed benefit for agitation, but QTc prolongation and worsened cognition may limit its use (p929).":["u29"],"copy|Sertraline — initial 25 mg, max 200 mg/day; despite early promise, not shown superior to placebo for depression in dementia (p929).":["u30"],"copy|Escitalopram — the cleaner S-enantiomer of citalopram; being studied for agitation (S-CitAD) (p930).":["u31"],"copy|Paroxetine — anticholinergic properties, should be avoided in older adults (p930); anticholinergic effects may increase delirium risk, and may cause withdrawal effects due to short half-life (p929).":["u32"],"copy|Mirtazapine — sedating at lower doses; in one RCT (24 vs 16) no benefit over placebo for sleep in dementia, with increased daytime sleepiness (p930).":["u33"],"copy|Bupropion — avoid with a history of seizures or psychotic symptoms (p930).":["u34"],"copy|Nortriptyline — therapeutic window 50–150 ng/mL; anticholinergic effects, hypotension, sedation (p929).":["u35"],"copy|Desipramine — the other preferred TCA in Table 60-9; therapeutic window 150–300 ng/mL (higher than nortriptyline's — don't conflate the two), similar side-effect profile (p929).":["u36"],"copy|TCAs overall are limited by a high adverse-event burden compared to SSRIs/SNRIs (p930).":["u37"],"copy|Benzodiazepines are used in 8.5% to 20% of AD patients despite weak efficacy evidence; avoid, except emergencies (severe agitation with risk of harm) or failure of alternative pharmacologic interventions; if used, sparingly, short-acting preferred; glucuronidated agents (oxazepam, temazepam, lorazepam) preferable with complex medical comorbidities — no active metabolites (p931).":["u38"],"copy|Meta-analyses: donepezil, galantamine, and memantine beat placebo in reducing emergence of NPS (modest vs neuroleptics); AChI + memantine combination beats monotherapy and placebo; not first-line for acute NPS of moderate or greater severity, but reasonable for chronic NPS (p931).":["u39"],"copy|Methylphenidate improved apathy in AD in randomized trials; modafinil did not in one small RCT (p931).":["u40"],"copy|A Cochrane review found low-certainty evidence that melatonin doses up to 10 mg may have little or no sleep benefit (p931); one small trial of melatonin 3 mg helped agitation/sundowning (p932); ramelteon showed no sleep benefit (p932).":["u41"],"copy|Anticonvulsants (\"mood stabilizers\") are generally disappointing: valproate is ineffective and poorly tolerated; carbamazepine helped in one small trial but is a potent enzyme inducer with marrow-toxicity risk (p930).":["u42"],"copy|The 2026 exam paper misspells drug names (OLAZAPINE, Quentapine, Bopropion) — these are Olanzapine, Quetiapine, and Bupropion; don't let the spelling throw you (see reproduced questions below).":["u45"],"recall|Recall":["u46"],"2026-exam-questions-for-this-chapter|2026 exam questions for this chapter":["u47"],"copy|א. An atypical antipsychotic such as OLAZAPINE (sic) at a low dose for a short time ← key":["u48"],"copy|ב. A benzodiazepine such as CLONEX for calming, long term":["u49"],"copy|ג. An antihistamine such as PROMETHAZINE before sleep":["u50"],"copy|ד. A typical antipsychotic such as HALOPERIDOL at a low dose, regularly":["u51"],"copy|א. Sertraline ← key":["u52"],"copy|ב. Quentapine (sic)":["u53"],"copy|ג. Bopropion (sic)":["u54"],"copy|ד. Amitriptyline":["u55"],"copy|Provide reminders, cues, or prompts":["u57"],"copy|Identify individuals if patient does not remember names or has aphasia":["u58"],"copy|Use environmental cues (eg, calendars, keeping blinds raised during the day, keeping lights off at night)":["u59"],"copy|Keep objects for individual tasks in separate, labeled containers":["u60"],"copy|Use simple visual reminders (eg, pictures, arrows pointing to the bathroom)":["u61"],"copy|Eliminate noise and distractions while patient is engaged in an activity":["u62"],"copy|Break each task into very simple steps":["u63"],"copy|Offer simple choices (no more than two at a time)":["u64"],"copy|Provide simple verbal commands, using only one or two steps at a time":["u65"],"copy|Remove unnecessary items from the room while patients is engaged in a task":["u66"],"copy|Provide daily, structured, and predictable routines":["u67"],"copy|Provide 24-hour supervision for the patient":["u68"],"copy|Place locks on all exits":["u69"],"copy|Develop an emergency plan. Inform neighbors, fire department, and police of the patient’s condition":["u70"],"copy|Provide patient with medical alert and safe return ID bracelets":["u71"],"copy|Encourage good sleep hygiene (avoid stimulating activity, reduce caffeinated and alcoholic beverages that may affect sleep, limit daytime napping)":["u72"],"copy|Create a quiet routine for bedtime":["u73"],"copy|Evaluate environment for modifiable elements, such as temperature, noise level, light, shadows, and bed comfort":["u74"],"copy|Use a relaxed and reassuring voice and light touch to help calm or redirect":["u75"],"copy|Avoid negative words and tone":["u76"],"copy|Allow patient sufficient time to respond to questions":["u77"],"copy|Help patient with aphasia find the words to express him/herself":["u78"],"copy|Allow extra time for tasks and activities":["u79"],"copy|Distract the patient as necessary":["u80","u87"],"copy|Understand that behaviors are not always intentional":["u81"],"copy|Create a structured, daily schedule that includes entertainment and exercise":["u82"],"copy|Provide meaningful activities that draw on preserved capabilities and interests":["u83"],"copy|Engage patients in activities involving repetitive motion (eg, folding towels, putting items in containers)":["u84"],"copy|Go along with patient’s point of view of what is true. Avoid arguing or trying to reason as this can make the situation worse":["u85"],"copy|Ignore symptoms that are not distressing the person":["u86"],"copy|Give the patient a job at home, such as folding laundry":["u88"],"copy|Focus on the process of doing things rather than the results":["u89"],"copy|Distract from an inappropriate topic by calmly and firmly changing the subject":["u90"],"copy|Avoid television shows with violence or sexual content":["u91"],"copy|CHAPTER 60s / HAZZARD":["u92"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 60 — Behavioral Symptoms of Dementia and Psychoactive Drug Therapy; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u93"],"copy|Epidemiology & framework":["u94"],"copy|Diagnosis & differential":["u95"],"copy|Management framework":["u96"],"copy|Drugs — antipsychotics (all carry a black-box mortality warning in dementia)":["u97"],"copy|Drugs — antidepressants":["u98"],"copy|Drugs — other / adjuncts":["u99"],"copy|4.":["u101"],"copy|How common are psychotic symptoms in AD versus in dementia with Lewy bodies or Parkinson disease dementia?":["u102"],"copy|20% to 30% in AD; over 50% in DLB/Parkinson disease dementia (p923).":["u103"],"copy|5.":["u104"],"copy|What does the DICE mnemonic for NPS management stand for?":["u105"],"copy|Describe, Investigate, Create, Evaluate (p924).":["u106"],"copy|7.":["u107"],"copy|What is first-line management of NPS in dementia, and what is the stated exception?":["u108"],"copy|Nonpharmacologic interventions; exception is emergency situations where the safety of the patient or others is compromised, eg, severe agitation (p925).":["u109"],"copy|8.":["u110"],"copy|Name the three exceptions that allow starting a psychotropic without first exhausting nonpharmacologic approaches.":["u111"],"copy|Clear-cut major depression; psychosis causing or risking harm; aggression causing or risking harm (p925).":["u112"],"copy|10.":["u113"],"copy|Per the AHRQ comparative-effectiveness review, for which targets were risperidone, olanzapine, and aripiprazole most effective?":["u114"],"copy|Risperidone for psychosis and agitation; olanzapine for agitation; aripiprazole for overall NPS (p928).":["u115"],"copy|11.":["u116"],"copy|Which antipsychotic's low EPS risk makes it a good option for psychotic symptoms in dementia with Lewy bodies, and what limits its use?":["u117"],"copy|Clozapine — limited by serious adverse effects (neutropenia/agranulocytosis) and close laboratory monitoring: weekly blood draws in the first 6 months (p928).":["u118"],"copy|13.":["u119"],"copy|In the CitAD trial, which SSRI improved agitation in Alzheimer disease, and what limited its use?":["u120"],"copy|Citalopram; limited by QTc prolongation and worsened cognition (p929).":["u121"],"copy|15.":["u122"],"copy|Benzodiazepines are used in what proportion of AD patients despite weak efficacy evidence, and which ones are preferred if used?":["u123"],"copy|8.5% to 20%; short-acting preferred, and glucuronidated agents — oxazepam, temazepam, or lorazepam — are preferable with complex medical comorbidities (p931).":["u124"],"copy|Q9 — sourced to p.":["u126"],"copy|925.":["u127"],"copy|A 90-year-old woman, known to have Alzheimer-type dementia for about 7 years.":["u128"],"copy|Her family report that she was seen in the emergency department because of behavioural disturbances manifested as restlessness, shouting and delusions.":["u129"],"copy|Delirium was ruled out, as were an infectious process, pain, urinary retention, constipation and metabolic disturbances.":["u130"],"copy|The family finds it hard to care for her.":["u131"],"copy|Behavioural-environmental treatment failed.":["u132"],"copy|What is the recommended approach to continuing treatment?":["u133"],"copy|\"OLAZAPINE\" is the paper's spelling; the book prints \"Olanzapine\" (Table 60-8, p928).":["u135"],"copy|Book: p925: \"Due to the inherent risks of using medications to treat NPS in dementia, nonpharmacologic approaches should be first-line therapy.":["u136"],"copy|Psychotropics should be used only after other efforts have been made to mitigate NPS, with three exceptions: (1) clear-cut major depression; (2) psychosis causing harm or with significant potential of harm to self or others; and (3) aggression causing harm or risk of harm to self or other.\"":["u137"],"copy|Outside the pages the examiners cited, p927: \"treatment should be initiated at a low dose and titrated to the minimum effective dose as tolerated.\" and \"if there is no significant response after a 4-week period, the medication should be tapered and withdrawn.\"":["u138"],"copy|Q52 — sourced to p.":["u139"],"copy|929.":["u140"],"copy|A 78-year-old man with ischaemic heart disease, CKD stage 3 and osteoarthritis, diagnosed with major depression.":["u141"],"copy|His PHQ-9 score is 17.":["u142"],"copy|He has marked anhedonia, psychomotor retardation and low energy.":["u143"],"copy|He reports mild anxiety, without panic attacks.":["u144"],"copy|There is no psychosis and no suicidality.":["u145"],"copy|He prefers drug treatment.":["u146"],"copy|Which drug treatment is the most appropriate choice for the depression he has?":["u147"],"copy|\"Quentapine\" and \"Bopropion\" are the paper's spellings; the book prints \"Quetiapine\" (Table 60-8, p928) and \"Bupropion\" (Table 60-9, p929).":["u149"],"copy|Book: p929: Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors \"are considered first-line therapy for late-life depression and are generally well tolerated compared to other classes of antidepressants such as TCAs.\"":["u150"],"copy|Table 60-9 (p929) lists sertraline under \"SELECTIVE SEROTONIN REUPTAKE INHIBITORS\" (initial daily dose 25 mg, maximum 200 mg).":["u151"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, p.926 · Read source":["u152"],"copy|Disorientation or confusion with person/object recognition":["u153"],"copy|Confusion/overwhelmed with tasks and environment":["u154"],"copy|Wandering or inability to respond appropriately to an emergency":["u155"],"copy|Nighttime wakefulness":["u156"],"copy|Anxiety, irritability, repetitive questioning, frustration":["u157"],"copy|Boredom and restlessness":["u158"],"copy|Delusions and hallucinations":["u159"],"copy|Apathy":["u160"],"copy|Disinhibition":["u161"],"copy|Answer":["u163","u164","u165","u166","u167","u168","u169","u170"],"copy|NPS are associated with higher mortality, faster cognitive decline and institutional placement (pp. 919–920).":["u5"],"copy|New-onset acute NPS work-up: CBC, chem panel, LFTs, UA/culture; consider TSH, B12, folate, tox screen, ECG; chest x-ray if respiratory signs; imaging particularly if focal signs; EEG if seizure suspected; CSF rarely needed — indicated if meningitis/encephalitis suspected (p922). Interpret urine findings in context. With delirium and bacteriuria alone, and no urinary symptoms, fever or hemodynamic instability, assess other causes and observe rather than treating bacteriuria. IDSA, recommendation V.1.":["u11"],"copy|Clozapine 6.25–75 mg/day — lower motor risk than potent dopamine blockers, but it can still cause serious harm. Evidence of benefit comes from Parkinson disease psychosis. Efficacy and tolerability specifically in DLB remain uncertain. Seek specialist input if persistent dangerous or severely distressing psychosis warrants a trial. DLB Consortium consensus. Use is limited by neutropenia/agranulocytosis — weekly blood draws in the first 6 months (p928).":["u27"],"copy|Escitalopram — the S-enantiomer of citalopram. S-CitAD, ongoing when p930 was written, later found no significant benefit for agitation at 12 weeks. S-CitAD trial.":["u31"],"copy|Mirtazapine — sedating at lower doses; in the sleep pilot, 8 received mirtazapine and 16 placebo (24 total). Nighttime sleep did not improve and daytime sleepiness increased. Page 930 misstates the treatment-group size. Scoralick et al., methods and results.":["u33"],"copy|Which antipsychotic has relatively low motor toxicity, and what limits extrapolating its Parkinson psychosis evidence to dementia with Lewy bodies?":["u117"],"copy|Clozapine has lower motor toxicity, but efficacy and tolerability in dementia with Lewy bodies are uncertain.":["u118"],"copy|Neutropenia can be life-threatening and requires absolute neutrophil count monitoring, typically weekly in the first six months (p928).":["u118"],"copy|Clozapine, 6.25–75 mg/day. Hazzard calls clozapine a good option for psychotic symptoms in DLB because it has a low risk of extrapyramidal effects. Neutropenia or agranulocytosis limits its use. The table specifies weekly blood draws during the first 6 months (p928).Current practice (not the book): The DLB Consortium cites benefit in Parkinson disease psychosis. Efficacy and tolerability in DLB itself remain unestablished. Fourth consensus report, 2017.":["u27"],"copy|Escitalopram is the S-enantiomer of citalopram. Hazzard describes S-CitAD as ongoing at press time (p930).Current practice (not the book): S-CitAD subsequently found no significant benefit for agitation at 12 weeks and observed drug-related QT prolongation. S-CitAD trial, 2025.":["u31"],"copy|Table 60-9 gives a maximum of 20 mg daily above age 60.":["u296"],"copy|Hazzard describes improved agitation, lower caregiver stress, better ADL performance and less rescue lorazepam with citalopram in CitAD (p929).":["u332"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): the published CitAD trial found improvement in selected agitation and caregiver-distress measures, with cognitive worsening and QT prolongation at its target of 30 mg/day.":["u333"],"copy|ADL performance and rescue lorazepam use did not differ significantly.":["u334"],"copy|S-CitAD found no significant improvement in agitation at 12 weeks with escitalopram and reported QT prolongation.":["u300"],"copy|CitAD trial, 2014, Table 2 · S-CitAD trial, 2025.":["u303"],"copy|Which drug does the chapter call a good option for psychotic symptoms in DLB, and which adverse effect limits its use?":["u335"],"copy|Hazzard names clozapine because of its low risk of extrapyramidal effects.":["u336"],"copy|Severe neutropenia requires close laboratory monitoring, with weekly blood draws for the first six months in the book’s table (p928).":["u118"],"copy|Current practice note.":["u337"],"copy|CHAPTER 60 / HAZZARD":["u92"],"copy|The drug tables give US doses and availability at publication (p928).":["u43"],"table-60-6|Table 60-6":["u56"],"copy|Exam traps":["u100"],"copy|These English translations cover the 2026 exam questions on dementia care.":["u125"],"copy|The chapter's own text lists cholinesterase-inhibitor side effects as \"eight loss\" — a printing error for \"weight loss\" (correctly spelled in Table 60-9, p929); p931 uses the misspelling.":["u44"],"copy|Exam spelling.":["u134","u148"],"copy|June 2026 Q9 (page citation)":["u162"],"behavioral-symptoms-of-dementia-and-psychoactive-drug-therapy|Behavioral Symptoms of Dementia and Psychoactive Drug Therapy":["u338"],"behavioral-syndromes-and-assessment|Behavioral syndromes and assessment":["u339"],"depression-apathy-and-other-mimics|Depression, apathy and other mimics":["u340"],"separate-major-depression-apathy-and-medication-toxicity|Separate major depression, apathy and medication toxicity":["u187"],"care-interactions-and-non-drug-treatment|Care interactions and non-drug treatment":["u341"],"medicines-benefits-and-harms|Medicines, benefits and harms":["u342"],"decide-whether-an-antipsychotic-trial-is-justified|Decide whether an antipsychotic trial is justified":["u186"],"key-numbers|Key numbers":["u343"],"israeli-context|Israeli context":["u188"],"test-yourself|Test yourself":["u344"],"copy|Identify what the behavior means to the patient before trying to suppress it.":["u345"],"copy|Pain, delirium, medicines and an unmet need can look similar.":["u346"],"copy|Depression, anxiety, apathy, hallucinations and disinhibition can emerge before or after cognitive decline becomes obvious.":["u189"],"copy|The chapter links these symptoms to vulnerability in brain circuits controlling behavior, compounded by illness, pain, sensory deficits and the demands of the surroundings.":["u190"],"copy|Their presence is associated with faster decline, greater caregiver burden and a greater likelihood of institutional placement.":["u191"],"copy|Improvement in one target symptom does not establish improvement in cognition or independence.":["u192"],"copy|Hazzard pp.":["u347","u350","u351","u352","u353","u357","u361","u362","u371"],"copy|919–920, 927–928.":["u194"],"copy|NPS are associated with higher mortality, faster cognitive decline and institutional placement (pp.":["u348"],"copy|919–920).":["u349"],"copy|Get the time course from someone who knows the person.":["u195"],"copy|Years of decline with wandering or social disinhibition can fit dementia.":["u196"],"copy|Marked new sleepiness over two days or rapidly changing agitation warrants an assessment for delirium, including attention, arousal, medication changes, pain and acute illness.":["u197"],"copy|Profound sadness and suicidal thoughts require separate urgent assessment.":["u198"],"copy|Use examination and focused tests to investigate plausible causes.":["u199"],"copy|The NPI-Q can describe symptoms and caregiver distress but cannot identify their cause.":["u200"],"copy|920–923.":["u202"],"copy|Ask what the hallucination means to this patient.":["u203"],"copy|Formed visions of people or animals, cognitive fluctuations, parkinsonism and dream enactment favor dementia with Lewy bodies.":["u204"],"copy|A calm person seeing something others cannot see may need reassurance and observation.":["u205"],"copy|Distressing or dangerous symptoms need further evaluation.":["u206"],"copy|Review agents such as amantadine that can worsen hallucinations.":["u207"],"copy|Donepezil or another cholinesterase inhibitor may help cognition and hallucinations.":["u208"],"copy|Dopamine-blocking antipsychotics can cause profound rigidity or a severe sensitivity reaction.":["u209"],"copy|Hazzard pp. 923–924, 928 and 984.":["u210"],"copy|Table 60-6":["u56"],"copy|Crying, withdrawal, irritability and new anxiety can represent depression, but apathy and cognitive decline can produce overlapping symptoms.":["u262"],"copy|Check duration, functional effect, enjoyment, sleep, appetite and suicidal thoughts with the patient and informant.":["u263"],"copy|A screening score signals the need for assessment.":["u264"],"copy|An SSRI such as escitalopram can target a depressive disorder when medication is warranted.":["u265"],"copy|Donepezil targets symptomatic Alzheimer disease.":["u266"],"copy|The June 2023 item accepted both choices.":["u267"],"copy|Antidepressant benefit for depression specifically in dementia is inconsistent, and NICE favors psychological approaches for mild to moderate depression in mild to moderate dementia.":["u268"],"copy|923, 928–931.":["u270"],"copy|NICE NG97, recommendations 1.7.11–1.7.12.":["u271"],"copy|For an older adult with major depression, mild anxiety and ischemic heart disease, sertraline is a reasonable first drug.":["u272"],"copy|It has little anticholinergic activity.":["u273"],"copy|Bupropion may be activating, and comorbid kidney disease calls for dose caution.":["u274"],"copy|Amitriptyline adds orthostasis and anticholinergic and cardiac effects.":["u275"],"copy|An antipsychotic is a poor initial choice for uncomplicated major depression.":["u276"],"copy|929–930.":["u278"],"copy|Think in mechanisms.":["u279"],"copy|Confusion with urinary retention suggests muscarinic blockade: paroxetine stands out among commonly used SSRIs, while escitalopram has relatively little anticholinergic activity.":["u280"],"copy|Amitriptyline and promethazine also add substantial burden.":["u281"],"copy|New confusion alone might reflect hyponatremia.":["u282"],"copy|Agitation accompanied by clonus, hyperreflexia and autonomic disturbance instead suggests serotonin toxicity, which can occur with citalopram especially alongside another serotonergic drug.":["u283"],"copy|922, 929–930, Tables 60-3 and 60-9.":["u285"],"copy|AGS Beers Criteria 2023, Table 7.":["u286"],"copy|Interpret urine findings in context.":["u354"],"copy|With delirium and bacteriuria alone, and no urinary symptoms, fever or hemodynamic instability, assess other causes and observe rather than treating bacteriuria.":["u355"],"copy|IDSA, recommendation V.1.":["u356"],"copy|SSRIs and SNRIs can lower serum sodium through inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion.":["u287"],"copy|Consider the drug and other contributors if new confusion or gait instability follows initiation.":["u288"],"copy|Both mirtazapine and bupropion were accepted as lower-risk options in the September item.":["u289"],"copy|Neither guarantees normal sodium.":["u290"],"copy|Mirtazapine can increase appetite and daytime sleepiness, even at a low dose.":["u291"],"copy|929–930, Table 60-9.":["u293"],"copy|Read the ECG from the beginning of QRS to the end of T.":["u294"],"copy|Citalopram prolongs ventricular repolarization in a dose-dependent manner.":["u295"],"copy|Hypokalemia and other QT-prolonging drugs increase concern.":["u297"],"copy|Current practice: Citalopram benefits and QT effects. the published CitAD trial found improvement in selected agitation and caregiver-distress measures, with cognitive worsening and QT prolongation at its target of 30 mg/day.":["u358"],"copy|Lithium and a new thiazide.":["u304"],"copy|Sodium loss can reduce renal lithium clearance.":["u305"],"copy|New tremor, falls, altered consciousness or clonus warrant prompt evaluation of lithium level, electrolytes and kidney function.":["u306"],"copy|A routine urine toxicology screen does not measure lithium.":["u307"],"copy|For agitation in Alzheimer disease, the completed Lit-AD trial found no significant advantage over placebo on its primary agitation and aggression measure.":["u308"],"copy|Hazzard p.":["u359","u360"],"copy|931.":["u310"],"copy|Lithium prescribing information, sections 5.1 and 7.1 · Lit-AD results":["u311"],"copy|During a difficult bath, ask the caregiver to describe the moment that went wrong, what preceded it, what the person did and how each responded.":["u211"],"copy|Investigate means looking for a cause that can change: painful movement, constipation, cold, fear, loss of privacy, unfamiliar instructions or a medication effect.":["u212"],"copy|Create a plan with the person, caregiver and team: try a familiar time, offer a choice, give one instruction at a time and pause when fear rises.":["u213"],"copy|Evaluate asks both whether the plan was tried and whether distress decreased.":["u214"],"copy|If the plan was impractical, find out why.":